  "body": "Automated worker task `task-20260808-112749-adopted-land-stop-work-order-gate--issue--980--u`. Risk tier: tier1.\n\nSuperboss review: The gate's structure (submit()-time check plus dispatch_one() defense-in-depth, tuple-of-order-ids, task_kind scoping limited to the only PR/push-capable kind, extensive tests) is sound, but its core security primitive does not deliver the guarantee its own docstring claims: _git_committed_file_text() reads `git show HEAD:<path>` in whatever branch happens to be checked out in the shared, live AI_OS working directory at call time -- it never verifies that HEAD is on the trunk branch (e.g. main) or has been pushed to/merged on origin. The docstring for this function explicitly asserts the fix works because a fabricated exemption 'once pushed, is caught by whatever review this repo already requires for that branch,' but the code enforces no such thing -- a purely local `git commit` (or simply `git checkout` onto a branch with a favorable commit already sitting on it) on the exact same laptop/server filesystem satisfies the check just as well as a reviewed, merged PR would, with zero GitHub involvement. This is not hypothetical: checking the live AI_OS repo right now (the same VERIDIAN_AI_OS_DIR/OWNER_DECISIONS_PATH default this code reads in production) shows it currently checked out on a non-main branch (docs/hard-rule3-correction-...) whose HEAD commit message is 'Owner: lift stop-work order (issue #980), decided directly on server' -- i.e. the exact ambient-HEAD instability this gate's whole design depends on not existing is the live, present state of the very file it reads. Given this PR's explicitly stated threat model is a 'compromised or fabricating' dispatched worker, and such a worker has the same filesystem/git access to AI_OS as anything else running there, this gap lets that same actor defeat the gate through a trivially easier route (local commit, no push, no PR, no review) than the one the code was written to close. Secondary, lower-severity: the 'stop-work-order-lifted' branch returns None (lifting the gate entirely) as soon as ANY approved entry contains that substring in its id, without checking which specific order in STOP_WORK_ORDER_TASK_IDS it corresponds to -- fine while only one order is ever open, but the tuple is explicitly documented to grow, and an entry meant to lift one order would silently lift all of them. Recommend a follow-up that pins the read to the actual trunk branch and/or verifies the commit is reachable from (or matches) origin/<trunk> before treating it as authoritative, plus scoping the lifted-check to a specific order id.",
